527	/* ======================================================================
528	 * Calibration-phase two-lane parity + pass-twice determinism model.
529	 *
530	 * This is the browser-measured falsification proof the in-contract calibrate
531	 * driver emits, and it is deliberately DISTINCT from the published-receipt run
532	 * model above. It asserts only what the running browser measured — the journey
533	 * legs, the recorded navigations, the tracked-event outcomes, the clean-page
534	 * counters, and the live-backend request category — and claims NO frozen SPA
535	 * byte inventory, because building that inventory is the follow-up receipt
536	 * phase, not the calibration phase.
537	 *
538	 * The two digests carry the two guarantees:
539	 *
540	 *   - behaviorDigest is the LANE-INDEPENDENT projection. It is what both lanes
541	 *     must share: routing, state round-trip, errors, console, failed requests,
542	 *     backend category, and tracked-event outcomes. Baseline and migrated
543	 *     reaching one behaviorDigest is the two-lane parity claim.
544	 *
545	 *   - semanticDigest is behaviorDigest PLUS the one legitimately per-lane fact —
546	 *     the lane's own production-bundle byte identity — and it is pass-INDEPENDENT
547	 *     within a lane. pass-1 semanticDigest == pass-2 semanticDigest on each lane
548	 *     is the determinism claim, and because the byte identity is folded in, the
549	 *     two lanes' semanticDigests DIFFER, which is what makes the parity of their
550	 *     behaviorDigests non-trivial: two genuinely different builds, one behavior.
551	 *
552	 * The per-lane byte identity is the only declared per-lane difference, and it is
553	 * kept OUT of behaviorDigest by construction. Nothing else is per-lane: the
554	 * measured DOM behaves identically across the two lanes.
555	 * ==================================================================== */
556	
